#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124276 :question/id #uuid "253b765d-be85-574e-8e32-c359ffbecdea" :question/math-academy-id "e-3694" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+6{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$  into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+6({e}^{t})^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+6{e}^{2t}\\text{ }j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }i+{e}^{t}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124277 :question/id #uuid "033e459e-5148-5e78-ab5c-7e455b06cd64" :question/math-academy-id "e-3695" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) =  t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t  \\, \\mathbf j +t^3  \\, \\mathbf k,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,k \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{xz}{y}\\,j+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2,$ $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{4})\\cdot 1+({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{4}+3{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & ={t}^{4}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124278 :question/id #uuid "f44b6ea6-694c-5f54-94ee-4c4a699bf4eb" :question/math-academy-id "e-3696" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t  \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ is given by $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] .$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t, y=\\cos t,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-1 \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124404 :question/id #uuid "c623bb85-401d-5d00-b4a8-cb86570d3304" :question/math-academy-id "e-8282" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x = \\sin{t}$ and $y = \\cos{t}$ into $\\nabla f(x,y)$ to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t = \\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(1)+0-(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124955 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d91284b-aa86-5efd-8e00-bc13b952dcba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}+2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321627} {:answer/id #uuid "57674ee3-9965-5502-a818-bbcc31f0235a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321835} {:answer/id #uuid "60879608-43b6-5c2d-8f50-f7877f002d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321904} {:answer/id #uuid "8305ad0c-8f86-50aa-a55a-367d449dabc4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}-2{e}^{-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322180} {:answer/id #uuid "e18c50bc-afa0-58da-b693-f0e3601201ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}+{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322910}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "60879608-43b6-5c2d-8f50-f7877f002d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321904} :answer-field/id #uuid "f71b9994-814b-5c24-af5b-f67cb4ffae0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323654}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5ea55a51-bc91-526a-9277-7689869bc683" :question/math-academy-id "q-36211" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{-t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y={e}^{-t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i-{e}^{-t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i-{e}^{-t}\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t}-2{e}^{-t}\\cdot {e}^{-t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124956 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05130439-f8ad-50ab-88fd-4aef2c512276" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321194} {:answer/id #uuid "2ab62fb4-96f6-544e-a43f-7c54848d7b96" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321475} {:answer/id #uuid "2bb77509-e2b1-57fe-aa81-c820e1759f9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321484} {:answer/id #uuid "dd29a8cb-f3e2-55a6-b61e-7dcb1a986936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322877} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8e8763-9fc2-54d7-beb9-a7f164362324" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322991}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dd29a8cb-f3e2-55a6-b61e-7dcb1a986936" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322877} :answer-field/id #uuid "b0f23e9e-f039-5d42-b46f-a3ba271cfc87" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323513}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "ceb16fee-64a8-51ee-a3b7-7009e970259a" :question/math-academy-id "q-36215" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that\n$$\n\\displaystyle ∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j\n$$\nand\n$r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{x}^{2}+3y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=t$ and $y={t}^{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(t)}{(t)^{2}+3({t}^{2})}\\,i+\\frac{3}{(t)^{2}+3({t}^{2})}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{3}{{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{4{t}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\,j)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2t}\\right)\\cdot 1+\\left(\\frac{3}{4{t}^{2}}\\right)\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}+\\frac{3}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{2}{1}=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d6b5248-8092-5223-a861-76c990d98151" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+(1+2t){e}^{t}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445647} {:answer/id #uuid "3f8c2a6a-498f-5431-bf0a-f09364aeca5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}+2e+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445649} {:answer/id #uuid "55795f5b-54c2-5a78-8f3f-d7bc330a0c55" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+(t+1){e}^{t}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445653} {:answer/id #uuid "88d607c8-fe3c-5a8f-95e2-26b4560aaab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+(1+t){e}^{t}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445659} {:answer/id #uuid "9a2dcd24-b4f5-5abd-afe6-bb8c91b06230" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+t{e}^{t}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445663}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "88d607c8-fe3c-5a8f-95e2-26b4560aaab5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t+1}+(1+t){e}^{t}+e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445659} :answer-field/id #uuid "00f9b55f-9f7a-5171-8a0f-7462d539b472" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122354}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f10e177e-2926-5cde-8e9a-4b0a99b2ff4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36238" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=xy+yz+xz$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+e\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xy+yz+xz)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xy+yz+xz)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xy+yz+xz)\\,k \\\\  & =(y+z)\\,i+(x+z)\\,j+(y+x)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y={e}^{t},$ and $z=e$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =({e}^{t}+e)\\,i+(t+e)\\,j+({e}^{t}+t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(e)\\,k \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t}\\,j+(0)\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(({e}^{t}+e)\\,i+(t+e)\\,j+({e}^{t}+t)\\,k)\\cdot (i+{e}^{t}\\,j+(0)\\,k) \\\\  & =({e}^{t}+e)\\cdot 1+(t+e)\\cdot {e}^{t}+({e}^{t}+t)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & ={e}^{t}+e+t{e}^{t}+{e}^{t+1} \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}+(1+t){e}^{t}+e.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124960 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bde96964-49e5-5f8c-9a85-bf83f3f624a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122361} {:answer/id #uuid "c18873f1-6416-5e3f-84a8-30f0d1e34a57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122362} {:answer/id #uuid "eb8b5412-a282-5231-a52a-c430c381a304" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122363} {:answer/id #uuid "55bd4f06-001b-5b40-9449-067e29d3fd21" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122364} {:answer/id #uuid "5c13ba27-5c90-535c-9ea9-9232444e86bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122365}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde96964-49e5-5f8c-9a85-bf83f3f624a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122361} :answer-field/id #uuid "30ce640c-974a-53b3-8a92-6e2b4d383ffe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122360}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "e234e1ce-e3a5-5460-9b85-a8876cb6e36c" :question/math-academy-id "q-36247" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+3xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},t+1,-t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+3xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+3xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+3xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x+3yz,3xz,3xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y=t+1,$ and $z=-t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2({t}^{2})+3(t+1)(-t),3({t}^{2})(-t),3({t}^{2})(t+1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2}-3{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,1,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-{t}^{2}-3t,-3{t}^{3},3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,1,-1⟩ \\\\  & =(-{t}^{2}-3t)\\cdot 2t+(-3{t}^{3})\\cdot 1+(3{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2})\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-2{t}^{3}-6{t}^{2}-3{t}^{3}-3{t}^{3}-3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =-8{t}^{3}-9{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =-8{(1)}^{3}-9{(1)}^{2}=-17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124961 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b632d6e-7a98-53fb-a62b-8c9001dbea44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122369} {:answer/id #uuid "019ba0e0-ece3-5798-adda-30d5f0d23288" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479360} {:answer/id #uuid "3c938f80-1a2e-5bdc-937f-bbe6aa683da7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479449} {:answer/id #uuid "5f8f5ab1-0a45-584c-b611-498bfb7e8229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479497} {:answer/id #uuid "ccd3d589-8795-5da6-b2f2-1c064d2c6d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5\\operatorname{cos}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5f8f5ab1-0a45-584c-b611-498bfb7e8229" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479497} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbe9c851-9ada-5f1b-ae5b-56517e51d11b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186122366}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8571bcaa-6cdf-5385-98c4-d1ed2624cfd7" :question/math-academy-id "q-36253" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2\\sqrt{t},4t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-y)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=4t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},4⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t),-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},4⟩ \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{t}-4t)(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}-4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\sqrt{1}-4\\cdot 1)(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}-4) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125246 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15d810bf-905d-5d78-ba48-12ddc9b892e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357945} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6ad6c1-0c8d-5fb0-b435-dee857f73211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358135} {:answer/id #uuid "7ae9204f-c34d-510e-94bc-023fcf5f25fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358819} {:answer/id #uuid "9af214c2-c6da-5e82-8df8-28a9a5b98d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359108} {:answer/id #uuid "acbb10f0-bfb6-5636-9744-b6306c87e6ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9af214c2-c6da-5e82-8df8-28a9a5b98d27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359108} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1da5512-5fc1-5bb7-8ab0-03b3b2b0ba70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360309}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "72c15c65-cd14-5a6c-9b4a-a63cd2f705de" :question/math-academy-id "q-94872" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+t\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\frac{t}{2}$ and $y=t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))] & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21d33325-0f7c-59d5-9622-3be484ea7f04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t+1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445645} {:answer/id #uuid "28207432-84ed-53b8-96d3-f43fe1450b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445646} {:answer/id #uuid "683e544e-ffb7-547d-87ef-b5301c6dac41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-t\\operatorname{sec}⁡t}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445656} {:answer/id #uuid "99330b27-129d-50df-97de-421d0fe9607b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t+1}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445661} {:answer/id #uuid "fa4236d2-abd7-5e33-9757-638b2e5b9cc5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+2}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28207432-84ed-53b8-96d3-f43fe1450b2c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2-t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445646} :answer-field/id #uuid "00f4d712-e22f-5cf5-b66e-67d08872e484" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124094}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "72c45146-95bd-53af-b2f0-49b5ef23b12e" :question/math-academy-id "q-95056" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡xy$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡xy)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{xy}\\,i+\\frac{x}{xy}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y={t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}+\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\  & =\\frac{2-t\\operatorname{tan}⁡t}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186125250 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f80eba8-e277-54b6-a822-397fce4bd264" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124103} {:answer/id #uuid "8ab97f80-a525-5bbe-a6b4-4f61835de51f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479565} {:answer/id #uuid "ae4d169e-765b-5d10-9ef3-ea60b0437938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479620} {:answer/id #uuid "e711ca65-092d-53a1-9492-fad3c11a523c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479701} {:answer/id #uuid "eef6c2ca-b384-52f9-bf10-2db701968ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479712}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eef6c2ca-b384-52f9-bf10-2db701968ad9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479712} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6d53ae6-99b0-5b9a-984f-d8cbdd57249f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186124100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "9127ceaf-3e13-50a4-b08a-89114324036d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95075" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=z(x+y)$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(z(x+y))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(z(x+y))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(z(x+y))\\,k \\\\  & =z\\,i+z\\,j+(x+y)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({e}^{t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1346c66e-add9-58bb-914d-bde230ffb97c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}+3t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281874} {:answer/id #uuid "5cbc6691-be3c-5174-bc33-891dad11a619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}+2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282476} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca6cbe6-66c9-5cdf-85f9-16bcc6c8db5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282765} {:answer/id #uuid "c8135d32-63d4-5f0e-ba7b-566e64774dee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283438} {:answer/id #uuid "d748975e-258c-5fd7-a8c3-a751942b65a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283565}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ca6cbe6-66c9-5cdf-85f9-16bcc6c8db5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282765} :answer-field/id #uuid "771db56a-a84d-5c67-ab37-e76a138f7e66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284138}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "78b6efe3-1fb5-5655-97e2-03def53db36e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167453" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3}$ and $r(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+\\,j+t\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & ={y}^{2}\\,i+(2xy+{z}^{3})\\,j+3y{z}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{3},y=1,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,k \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}i+(0)\\,j+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\,i+3{t}^{3}\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}i+(0)\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{3}\\cdot 0+3{t}^{2}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}+3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284585 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "439aee45-933e-5525-9345-5b17029a63c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282266} {:answer/id #uuid "582ea5c1-1e57-5176-9167-972093895fd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282440} {:answer/id #uuid "7eb3e4fe-ec5b-5339-9652-4a5cb0a96a8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282782} {:answer/id #uuid "848c5f97-904d-5825-a1c2-c3a1c330760f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282831} {:answer/id #uuid "e9802ab6-19b7-5148-b0e2-623781519ded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283729}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "848c5f97-904d-5825-a1c2-c3a1c330760f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282831} :answer-field/id #uuid "353e1da0-d350-56e5-9994-3f64a6bca475" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284029}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d2df41f1-db14-5e76-bbfb-6cc0c8cd31a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-167497" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\pi $ given that $∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=\\frac{t}{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j) \\\\  & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\pi ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (-1)+0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29453b2c-6d17-5788-a24a-a3018b5c97bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282046} {:answer/id #uuid "2c1dd875-1ab5-5d5c-8cee-d4a760e0fad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282077} {:answer/id #uuid "36e21432-61e2-5f75-bf7b-6c17d3bac35a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282176} {:answer/id #uuid "878e3c0b-0fa7-530a-8b50-3b0123c90209" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282857} {:answer/id #uuid "f3ea2448-8707-56af-883d-3fdfe6fbad5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283819}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "29453b2c-6d17-5788-a24a-a3018b5c97bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282046} :answer-field/id #uuid "99251eef-a601-5418-8667-74393704a93e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284194}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "15c14f08-def7-5eb6-8ced-3b42bca65403" :question/math-academy-id "q-167498" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=-1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=t+{cos}^{2}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}\\cdot (1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}-2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-1))] & ={e}^{-1+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284587 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "945b9c04-6a85-5d2f-aac7-20fb300b68cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282964} {:answer/id #uuid "a0759ec4-44c2-5df4-8049-d2141efec4e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283086} {:answer/id #uuid "cefe4dd8-87f4-5862-998f-f2f8be85aafb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283496} {:answer/id #uuid "ec5f7e03-4f52-5b2c-83bb-c9656091962b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283757} {:answer/id #uuid "f0bb78c7-3837-59e6-b577-9cf297b1d8d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283790}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec5f7e03-4f52-5b2c-83bb-c9656091962b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283757} :answer-field/id #uuid "d53902b6-01db-5851-b51a-31934c273c12" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284305}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b00d973d-5785-5a66-98c1-0a412869e903" :question/math-academy-id "q-167499" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t}⟩$ at the point $t=9.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right),\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\left(\\frac{x}{y}\\right)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y},-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=\\sqrt{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(9))] & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323770 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11a620de-8bd0-57cc-9ca8-627d570b10b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321285} {:answer/id #uuid "539a1518-741f-5932-ab88-ffb0b81d4bb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321805} {:answer/id #uuid "6d69e5bf-08b0-519c-bd8e-5f0d93d50b2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321997} {:answer/id #uuid "ac78bbbd-2aa6-5d72-9963-b167b6edf486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322497} {:answer/id #uuid "c761e53e-5917-50ee-b4b8-03842b140243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322693}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac78bbbd-2aa6-5d72-9963-b167b6edf486" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322497} :answer-field/id #uuid "5a86fdda-9d41-5703-bec7-0aa41e4dbc4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323345}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0c3c463e-b1e6-5da9-a9e8-9ab02a63ca2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-36227" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{x}^{2},2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] & =3{sin}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323878 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1ec52e-ccdc-5b0f-b240-92720661c62f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+9{t}^{3}+6{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321508} {:answer/id #uuid "5c35907b-6462-5098-94af-12ec14663f58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-3t-3t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321877} {:answer/id #uuid "82fd3b74-e5d5-5eb9-84d2-cb77ba0c33b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-3{t}^{2}+36{t}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322179} {:answer/id #uuid "ac96cf45-01f8-5f94-b48d-b88b9ae7af6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{2}-12{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322499} {:answer/id #uuid "d89116e6-d4ee-5b61-abf5-08714b411c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322842}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d89116e6-d4ee-5b61-abf5-08714b411c74" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322842} :answer-field/id #uuid "d379d752-f7ce-576e-9786-94b4166ca354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a1916a81-682a-57cf-b7a5-c43c18c456ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-37099" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=2x-x{y}^{2}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+3{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2x-x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =(2-{y}^{2})\\,i-2xy\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=3{t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\,i-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+6t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2-9{t}^{4})\\,i-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+6t\\,j) \\\\  & =(2-9{t}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+(-6{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 6t \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{t}-9{t}^{3}-36{t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b203b6e-7830-50da-aa18-f454f862a948" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321238} {:answer/id #uuid "2cf6d8fc-e1b8-5fdc-b9b5-09f8f6941c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321497} {:answer/id #uuid "c175b73f-e2fb-54e8-a7e0-b2906ff65bfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +1}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322651} {:answer/id #uuid "c8d08b91-916d-5adb-a62d-964ea2cc4e5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi +2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322710} {:answer/id #uuid "e1542b36-0e72-5814-8c1f-4765946bc3e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{\\pi +1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322908}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2cf6d8fc-e1b8-5fdc-b9b5-09f8f6941c20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321497} :answer-field/id #uuid "276fef95-8127-5751-a8f3-63d27774306c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323248}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fdba027b-12bc-5b22-aa20-f040a6af0b36" :question/math-academy-id "q-37106" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=(2{t}^{2}+2)\\,i+\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2{t}^{2}+2$ and $y=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}+2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =4t\\,i+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\,i+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot (4t\\,i+\\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2{t}^{2}+2}\\cdot 4t+\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{{t}^{2}+1}+\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t+1}{({t}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{2(1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+1}{({1}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1} \\\\  & =\\frac{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+1)}{(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c36ce21-00fb-5fff-a854-09a22fe9b12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321487} {:answer/id #uuid "3e8cc371-8d3d-50ee-84ac-7aa30e752029" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321639} {:answer/id #uuid "51cf7225-599b-5037-bcde-98e28ee76a46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321791} {:answer/id #uuid "842b9eff-5030-5ffd-9f05-2ac210a4ff66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322191} {:answer/id #uuid "f84babd5-90be-52b2-8398-56d622fd27c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323099}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f84babd5-90be-52b2-8398-56d622fd27c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323099} :answer-field/id #uuid "5b9f0d4f-747d-5dbd-9a61-61b8b32ac61a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323349}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "48ca6f9a-7d23-5701-9123-39d54477cf36" :question/math-academy-id "q-37109" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=0$ given that $∇f(x,y)=y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j$ and $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={e}^{2t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}+1)\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(2t+1)\\,i+2t{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(2t+1)\\,i+2t{e}^{2t}\\,j)\\cdot (2{e}^{2t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}[(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =2{e}^{2(0)}((2(0)+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0))+2(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(0)))=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360524 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "336ae663-8acc-530e-96cc-0c9939efc8b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358206} {:answer/id #uuid "4e8323a2-f87f-54c1-ba41-37494660f68d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3e+{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358429} {:answer/id #uuid "68b9473e-f106-5375-ac81-7275f6b0a9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358670} {:answer/id #uuid "734c0594-8b9d-5fed-bb61-015770db6c72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358754} {:answer/id #uuid "f34de6af-084c-5ec3-8d75-77cbd5aa09e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "68b9473e-f106-5375-ac81-7275f6b0a9eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{-2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358670} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e56fa10-b537-572f-8386-98aeb21d532f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "97fd3ba8-8ab5-5d07-adaa-98357dd8d4e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-94873" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=2x\\,i+2y\\,j$ and $r(t)={e}^{-t}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2x\\,i+2y\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={e}^{-t}$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2{e}^{-t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =(-{e}^{-t})\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{-t}\\,i+2{e}^{t}\\,j)\\cdot ((-{e}^{-t})\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{-t}\\cdot (-{e}^{-t})+2{e}^{t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}-2{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =2{e}^{2(1)}-2{e}^{-2(1)} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2}-2{e}^{-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360525 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2963cff8-a340-5205-a3e9-f39819e56fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358130} {:answer/id #uuid "4b15c439-f511-541c-b00a-276ff973830a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358394} {:answer/id #uuid "78ff0f13-6c5c-58bf-8edf-2283322de497" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358802} {:answer/id #uuid "a0b339d0-3302-5c8a-b8b0-29fc07054890" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359154} {:answer/id #uuid "f3204c09-87cb-5a4b-9803-80dcb1e84a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2963cff8-a340-5205-a3e9-f39819e56fcc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358130} :answer-field/id #uuid "a36cec20-678c-5599-8ac5-1c5bef7d549d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360277}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f3d7bbdd-6122-519c-8393-02c2402f7ab7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94878" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j$ and $r(t)=2\\sqrt{t}\\,i-2\\sqrt{t}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=-2\\sqrt{t}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-(-2\\sqrt{t})\\operatorname{sin}⁡[(2\\sqrt{t})(-2\\sqrt{t})]\\,i-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡[(2\\sqrt{t})(-2\\sqrt{t})]\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\sqrt{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\right)j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\right)i) \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\right) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{8}\\right))] & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360526 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65cdcc58-be4b-5338-b00a-ddcb5371bd93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358643} {:answer/id #uuid "6b40bd8f-7feb-594d-94b7-c6fe911edb9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358691} {:answer/id #uuid "743c9228-cbd8-5669-88ae-262b1ab32094" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358757} {:answer/id #uuid "93cbeb11-d6d7-50dd-9acd-c56791d17285" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359047} {:answer/id #uuid "bd23f796-bcb5-5c88-998f-bd753a03f298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359399}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b40bd8f-7feb-594d-94b7-c6fe911edb9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358691} :answer-field/id #uuid "e85aac1a-a384-579c-bfda-1155a57b6d9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360406}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "8defdc8d-5773-5ad6-96bf-f8efe589fed9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94884" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t-1⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=2t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2{t}^{2},-2(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},2-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},2-4t⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,2⟩ \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t+(2-4t)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-8t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =4(0)^{3}-8(0)+4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360527 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36ecb641-5c9e-59d2-9af0-e8b4b2b12cda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358237} {:answer/id #uuid "3913aa2e-745b-50de-97ac-427a88ec41d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358256} {:answer/id #uuid "58cc3bc9-ecef-54de-9839-9c06a26426b5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358521} {:answer/id #uuid "996b9c09-c035-53fe-ba0a-1216f4518389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359098} {:answer/id #uuid "dd14b83a-9e9f-5129-b7b0-19250eeeb3f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "996b9c09-c035-53fe-ba0a-1216f4518389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359098} :answer-field/id #uuid "31bbcbbe-389e-5798-9aef-8032762b891a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360071}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "53f0d6f9-0bd0-5fbc-870f-a5ea8fa6cdba" :question/math-academy-id "q-94887" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=xyz$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t},3t-1⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xyz),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xyz)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨yz,xz,xy⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t,y=\\sqrt{t}$ and $z=3t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\sqrt{t}(3t-1),2t(3t-1),2t\\sqrt{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨(3t-1)\\sqrt{t},6{t}^{2}-2t,2t\\sqrt{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨(3t-1)\\sqrt{t},6{t}^{2}-2t,2t\\sqrt{t}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}},3⟩ \\\\  & =(3t-1)\\sqrt{t}\\cdot 2+(6{t}^{2}-2t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+2t\\sqrt{t}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6t\\sqrt{t}-2\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}-t}{\\sqrt{t}}+6t\\sqrt{t} \\\\  & =12t\\sqrt{t}-2\\sqrt{t}+\\frac{3{t}^{2}-t}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =12(1)\\sqrt{1}-2\\sqrt{1}+\\frac{3(1)^{2}-1}{\\sqrt{1}} \\\\  & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360528 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05e78089-dc3f-5261-a0b9-838d20687d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357803} {:answer/id #uuid "50e71c08-98f9-50a2-a32b-d087d082f280" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358447} {:answer/id #uuid "7c9f48cc-cae9-5cde-af64-d389fb6174af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358832} {:answer/id #uuid "b8de77d8-406c-5b9b-a817-38720fcff979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359354} {:answer/id #uuid "eec515f8-b1e7-52bb-b00a-3fe712040562" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359881}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8de77d8-406c-5b9b-a817-38720fcff979" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359354} :answer-field/id #uuid "c4f2da6b-5bbf-5958-acfc-6db810c9ab3f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360333}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "102aa53b-1989-56be-b06a-9a6f6770770c" :question/math-academy-id "q-94888" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,4,\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2},3{y}^{2},2xz⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y=4$ and $z=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3})^{2},3(4)^{2},2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3,48,2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3,48,2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}}⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-3)\\cdot 1+48\\cdot 0+2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-3+0+2{t}^{2} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =3(2)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =12-3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360529 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b55c69-c88a-5b54-9cd7-12d2acc90eba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358067} {:answer/id #uuid "46655cc2-46c6-57d9-aae7-530ec487a9e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358354} {:answer/id #uuid "5de3fd27-1a74-5e78-9c11-0753f0b2af77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{17}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358573} {:answer/id #uuid "97ab5991-5eae-5b87-9b48-34164ebe108a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359079} {:answer/id #uuid "a69903fd-123e-53f6-9cf8-6199c101e2e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359192}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "97ab5991-5eae-5b87-9b48-34164ebe108a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359079} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f8f9bfb-8eb2-5607-8ba8-b9429f52c201" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360026}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "530f5767-4f31-5ef7-a6dd-bdeac420a724" :question/math-academy-id "q-94891" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-1,3,2t⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t-1,$ $y=3$ and $z=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}},\\frac{2(3)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}},\\frac{2(2t)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 1+\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 0+\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-2+8t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{10t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =\\frac{10(0)-2}{5(0)^{2}-2(0)+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360544 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0661fd29-a292-5ab4-b31a-2396be427221" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357808} {:answer/id #uuid "2c19e560-7929-538c-bcc2-9884cff52e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358147} {:answer/id #uuid "bb354d15-c911-57ac-961b-2bf623e8c47c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359378} {:answer/id #uuid "bbd453d2-7215-5f3b-8c26-6cee0b42f4db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359383} {:answer/id #uuid "caaabb05-4b0e-56ee-9471-80ef684ce597" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2c19e560-7929-538c-bcc2-9884cff52e44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358147} :answer-field/id #uuid "3c5c1098-6416-500c-946c-2648c90f4466" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360078}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4eac0cd5-9d30-54ff-89b8-2cfd1b9003d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-95052" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $r(t)=\\pi t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j \\\\  & =-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\pi t$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\pi t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\pi \\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t\\,j)\\cdot (\\pi \\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =-\\pi {e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi t-\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360545 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e56dc23-76eb-5db9-9fce-e4d36ef6a300" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3({t}^{2}+1)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358426} {:answer/id #uuid "50d39335-f934-5320-9bd6-5f69635a09d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}{e}^{t}+2}{{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358445} {:answer/id #uuid "7dab4a7a-ef04-5286-8145-c994f7fd0f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358847} {:answer/id #uuid "de80fcbe-829a-5dae-a81d-fb3cf700a555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t(t-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359706} {:answer/id #uuid "eb612ba8-e57f-520d-9b63-e9ab14d4e478" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(t{e}^{t}-1)}{{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359849}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7dab4a7a-ef04-5286-8145-c994f7fd0f0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358847} :answer-field/id #uuid "5ed81ec3-d906-5a86-bc7c-31b1ba6834fb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360140}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "99c33c9d-4cdd-5e75-a91b-a13201898eee" :question/math-academy-id "q-95054" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={t}^{-2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x{y}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{x{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{x{y}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{2}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{-2}$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{-2}}\\,i+\\frac{2}{{e}^{t}}\\,j \\\\  & ={t}^{2}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{-2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =-2{t}^{-3}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}\\,i+2{e}^{-t}\\,j)\\cdot (-2{t}^{-3}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =-2({t}^{2})({t}^{-3})+2{e}^{-t}({e}^{t}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{2}{t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(t-1)}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360547 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20f3235b-60e2-5c23-a6d1-2912bfc76c91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358041} {:answer/id #uuid "b7fdac84-5c94-5bcb-847f-c1a99a8c5411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359347} {:answer/id #uuid "bde855a4-7fc2-52c3-af6f-b2e1fd0d6c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359405} {:answer/id #uuid "dc8b8503-66f4-52dd-b74c-bee16b502323" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+1)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359689} {:answer/id #uuid "e953648a-9a4f-5892-9fbd-2c82540d2fdb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359825}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bde855a4-7fc2-52c3-af6f-b2e1fd0d6c05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359405} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b3efa02-f1ae-5df0-98fd-0df55d2c7698" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360170}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "9d03333b-22a3-579a-b479-87085056a6c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95059" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡xy)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{x}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}+\\frac{{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}{1+{e}^{2t}{sin}^{2}⁡t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360548 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "043ac664-b524-524e-aa72-bf1d30123527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357793} {:answer/id #uuid "af3ddaa4-8953-5d46-9032-e2f95ff616b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t{cos}^{3}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359275} {:answer/id #uuid "c43dfc74-6743-5922-a14e-6c2caaeb73de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t{sin}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359473} {:answer/id #uuid "cc97c0a8-0127-5bdc-a492-17ba4f68be95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359546} {:answer/id #uuid "da9c33c8-b2a5-5f98-b17d-5810e16dbeda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{cos}^{2}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359665}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "043ac664-b524-524e-aa72-bf1d30123527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357793} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9feea77-0770-57d9-9bdc-bf443c95c674" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360439}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a60dcabe-5a9f-584e-9268-2f3213ceda5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-95070" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)={x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j+2t\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4}-\\operatorname{cos}⁡z)\\,k \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,$ $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $z=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j+2\\,k) \\\\  & =4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360550 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1347f5a2-01c0-5c7b-b717-680930abc12b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t{e}^{t}+1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357914} {:answer/id #uuid "4c03b829-963e-5a53-bccb-1865320c6ba0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}+1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358409} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8b2be7-935d-58f9-a8d2-f58e5e602a05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{t-1}(1-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358993} {:answer/id #uuid "a48fbdf1-c888-5cc7-ad1f-26a035cc7e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t+1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359180} {:answer/id #uuid "aa082587-0924-59bd-be2d-48ce5c0b988c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a48fbdf1-c888-5cc7-ad1f-26a035cc7e15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t+1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359180} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c6ee750-c6b9-5399-bdf4-e7500571e2b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360267}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "75389de5-3518-524a-bf8a-ad60601e753c" :question/math-academy-id "q-95072" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{t-1}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{yz}{xyz}\\,i+\\frac{xz}{xyz}\\,j+\\frac{xy}{xyz}\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j+\\frac{1}{z}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,y={e}^{t-1},$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t-1}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t-1})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =i+{e}^{t-1}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t-1}}\\,j+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,k)\\cdot (i+{e}^{t-1}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}+\\frac{{e}^{t-1}}{{e}^{t-1}}+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}+2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2t+1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360577 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28d173fc-2ace-5c23-8e6c-2e1100a8bcd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t(t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358124} {:answer/id #uuid "55409a0b-0c25-504a-bd65-c0781332f04d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+4t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358492} {:answer/id #uuid "ce0052e5-1ee4-5d5c-9667-77f78de9d0a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(t-\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359564} {:answer/id #uuid "ee064399-1401-555f-b6c0-3013abc3be94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t(t-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359872} {:answer/id #uuid "f41185b3-09b1-59b6-88eb-3c7555fb8f88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359919}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f41185b3-09b1-59b6-88eb-3c7555fb8f88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359919} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6daa545-65f3-5e47-b745-8aab21ef0ff5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360290}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ce5cc103-892b-5471-8317-cbb325d2454d" :question/math-academy-id "q-95142" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+(t+1)\\,j+(t-1)\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}[x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})]\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}[x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})]\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}[x({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})]\\,k \\\\  & =({y}^{2}-{z}^{2})\\,i+2xy\\,j-2xz\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,$ $y=t+1$ and $z=t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =[(t+1)^{2}-(t-1)^{2}]i+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot (t+1)\\,j-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot (t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =4t\\,i+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+\\,j+\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4t\\,i+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,j-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{t}\\,i+\\,j+\\,k) \\\\  & =4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot 1-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =4+2(t+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t-2(t-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & =4+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & =4(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "67fb2e01-985f-513d-813b-cfde3f1b377e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358661} {:answer/id #uuid "788c776c-5597-51d2-9394-be9dfb7f75ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358792} {:answer/id #uuid "901c96ff-6cee-55e9-8211-cd0101addfb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359007} {:answer/id #uuid "a22be918-009f-5e59-9d70-9b6808c8792a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359167} {:answer/id #uuid "cbc10872-0c59-572a-92f2-5c00d7f80870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359542}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cbc10872-0c59-572a-92f2-5c00d7f80870" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359542} :answer-field/id #uuid "fef695bf-af69-5590-8e72-7ce89ea59101" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360451}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "ee940918-1cef-5c23-a56c-5afe1494fd57" :question/math-academy-id "q-95146" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x$ and $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)\\,k \\\\  & =-yz\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,i+z\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,j+y\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y={e}^{t},$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}\\,i+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\,k)\\cdot (2t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{t}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}+2{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡{t}^{2}-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5305687e-83d8-5c87-af7d-a5b0a362d788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡t+{e}^{{t}^{2}}+t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358466} {:answer/id #uuid "631de459-4352-5ad1-949e-8ac23d4b20f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{csc}⁡t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358613} {:answer/id #uuid "b15809fa-40bc-5563-9a51-d40e84efc938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359292} {:answer/id #uuid "c66b80bb-3662-52ab-bf2e-dab3fb4673d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+{e}^{{t}^{2}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359496} {:answer/id #uuid "e0073e63-c4b1-5f0a-b806-ed90e183c49f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{tan}⁡t+\\frac{{e}^{{t}^{2}}}{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359718}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b15809fa-40bc-5563-9a51-d40e84efc938" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359292} :answer-field/id #uuid "d86a4006-7e81-5ee9-a91c-4ead86d71714" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360371}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "312e3468-92a5-5996-9fc8-ac654be83975" :question/math-academy-id "q-95149" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(xz)+{e}^{y})\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x}\\,i+{e}^{y}\\,j+\\frac{1}{z}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,y={t}^{2},$ and $z=\\frac{1}{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\,j+t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\,j+t\\,k)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+2t\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{t}{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+2t{e}^{{t}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445677 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05ab9250-28af-5908-8d6f-09833d521f51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445643} {:answer/id #uuid "67d5968e-4c9e-5d9b-9b55-ddaa5793f241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445655} {:answer/id #uuid "bd8e760c-2dd0-5dcf-8836-3c5e2deced71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445664} {:answer/id #uuid "cc6cc197-cc16-54f4-b4ca-9fa9bd9e20c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445666} {:answer/id #uuid "de9daefe-3ccb-54d5-9516-f29765d78d78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445668}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "67d5968e-4c9e-5d9b-9b55-ddaa5793f241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445655} :answer-field/id #uuid "da5c7c2a-000b-5483-8ada-2cf63e301ba8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445675}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "021b7b63-3816-5a7a-9f77-baf36bf193bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-65864" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle ∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j\n$$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,i+\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+\\frac{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445678 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c059460-c4c2-55a2-8585-7e51d8edae78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445644} {:answer/id #uuid "47522fd9-62bd-5474-804d-67ac7a770b4b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{sin}^{4}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445650} {:answer/id #uuid "4b40ea9e-cf34-5238-9134-0581b982e962" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445651} {:answer/id #uuid "c7da010b-a10a-5868-8d20-e4e46c3b4c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445665} {:answer/id #uuid "e85749fe-f788-582a-a8ba-6a6fb947faa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{cos}^{3}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7da010b-a10a-5868-8d20-e4e46c3b4c9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445665} :answer-field/id #uuid "47babdd0-5f4c-5c9a-9ce1-4d934697545f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445673}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "240fc49a-d022-5d2c-b6ad-58058500b98b" :question/math-academy-id "q-95051" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{4}-{y}^{4}$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{4}-{y}^{4})\\,j \\\\  & =4{x}^{3}\\,i-4{y}^{3}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(4{cos}^{3}⁡t\\,i-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{cos}^{3}⁡t-4{sin}^{3}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445679 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a36a033-d9b0-5aa7-a7f4-b5451390ac7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445648} {:answer/id #uuid "60dab585-b4f4-59ea-8d98-9a5182b158eb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445654} {:answer/id #uuid "8d6d49e7-278c-5501-995f-670dd3f19484" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2+2t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445660} {:answer/id #uuid "d82ae990-52e1-5862-b9ec-b3ce7c4bd555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445667} {:answer/id #uuid "ff848b94-7fca-53f6-95e5-3ab48c4f01c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445672}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d82ae990-52e1-5862-b9ec-b3ce7c4bd555" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445667} :answer-field/id #uuid "88e49b12-6312-563d-bb5a-a9112af44abf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445674}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "430b6986-f30c-500a-b5e9-219dd0f3ace5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167463" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+2t\\,j+\\frac{1}{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xyz))\\,k \\\\  & =yz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,i+xz\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,j+xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xyz)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y=2t$ and $z=\\frac{1}{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right)\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,i+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,k)\\cdot (\\,i+2\\,j-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,k) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 1+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445680 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bc8881d-ce79-5fa4-94b3-93194df95932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445652} {:answer/id #uuid "76ff76d1-a44d-5785-aeb6-f49cd08b8d01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445657} {:answer/id #uuid "869d2a2c-eca1-5a81-9c8c-bd38ea652d36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445658} {:answer/id #uuid "9980bb90-a6ef-51f4-88b3-4812a2e11e8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡(2\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445662} {:answer/id #uuid "e898d08a-c5cb-5c48-be98-c6dd2a755c94" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{ln}^{2}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445670}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bc8881d-ce79-5fa4-94b3-93194df95932" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{ln}^{2}⁡(\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445652} :answer-field/id #uuid "e6ea0794-709e-5625-aee2-f32cf13014aa" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186445676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "b98d6bad-fbc9-5978-8040-a1a35d995f54" :question/math-academy-id "q-36234" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,2t⟩$ at the point $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,{x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $y=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t,{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\frac{1}{t},2⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{t}+2{ln}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\pi }{\\pi }+2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi  \\\\  & =2{ln}^{2}⁡\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1600e78f-067b-5159-9df5-0a07460bd35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{-1}+3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479394} {:answer/id #uuid "4783f08a-d1dc-5e36-a5c3-01e18e5e73b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479463} {:answer/id #uuid "5983345c-db95-5ace-9463-67aa96a3fe71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4e+7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479485} {:answer/id #uuid "6acc7f99-e086-5514-b8e6-83a4c9ef8720" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479518} {:answer/id #uuid "97ef8f60-6130-5ffd-8c09-836902b3e0e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3-2{e}^{-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4783f08a-d1dc-5e36-a5c3-01e18e5e73b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479463} :answer-field/id #uuid "35243cd2-2483-5ad0-842a-67891f04a244" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479761}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "0ccfc773-296b-50db-8da8-c0588c64c45e" :question/math-academy-id "q-94871" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=-1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+(x{e}^{xy}+2y)\\,j$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=y{e}^{xy}\\,i+(x{e}^{xy}+2y)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=t+1$ and $y=t-1$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(t-1){e}^{(t+1)(t-1)}\\,i+((t+1){e}^{(t+1)(t-1)}+2(t-1))\\,j \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}\\,i+((t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,j \\\\  & =i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((t-1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}\\,i+((t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2)\\,j)\\cdot (i+j) \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}\\cdot 1+((t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =(t-1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+(t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2 \\\\  & =2t{e}^{{t}^{2}-1}+2t-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-1))] & =2(-1){e}^{(-1)^{2}-1}+2(-1)-2 \\\\  & =-2-2-2 \\\\  & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479841 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49a16002-fb70-57d8-b56c-e846ae55b5f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479469} {:answer/id #uuid "800531bb-e5bf-57d0-94df-bfb46de70ee9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(2{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479550} {:answer/id #uuid "87ac30df-06f9-515c-b483-8fa371168b08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({t}^{2}-1)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479560} {:answer/id #uuid "c3353af3-19ba-5e14-a733-a22abed74ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479654} {:answer/id #uuid "eaa24879-c05e-53a3-b066-9afb3802f261" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3353af3-19ba-5e14-a733-a22abed74ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479654} :answer-field/id #uuid "65e8ce36-6bdd-5ffc-86d9-2cca52842880" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479779}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "23fc0071-5d5d-57b9-949a-5e0f696a08ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-95074" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2}$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i+(t-1)\\,j+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+2{z}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =2x\\,i+2y\\,j+4z\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t+1,$ $y=t-1$ and $z=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(t+1)\\,i+2(t-1)\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,k \\\\  & =i+j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2(t+1)\\,i+2(t-1)\\,j+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (i+j+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,k) \\\\  & =2(t+1)\\cdot 1+2(t-1)\\cdot 1+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t+2+2t-2+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =4(t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479842 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189f09ce-2bb2-5efc-97b0-05b2a4b3fbb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{t}+t}{t{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479398} {:answer/id #uuid "5b802ceb-0898-5764-b40e-d7ea6a72486c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479489} {:answer/id #uuid "629e7fba-04e6-54e1-8d56-6ef59c0da0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}+3}{{e}^{t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479503} {:answer/id #uuid "65254441-20bf-539f-a01a-719a7b06e2d2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t{e}^{t}+3}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479506} {:answer/id #uuid "b44d800e-4a42-5329-8b39-02e75b4ca367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t+3}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479628}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b44d800e-4a42-5329-8b39-02e75b4ca367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t+3}{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479628} :answer-field/id #uuid "306c4e3a-7620-56d0-8ab5-267aba392210" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2ad8b1a6-b8e1-5739-bfbc-4544e317c6e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-95055" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{3}y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3{x}^{2}y}{{x}^{3}y}\\,i+\\frac{{x}^{3}}{{x}^{3}y}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{x}\\,i+\\frac{1}{y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t$ and $y={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{t}\\,i+\\frac{1}{{e}^{t}}\\,j)\\cdot (\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}+\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{e}^{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{t}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{t+3}{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479852 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "10678e1e-886a-5a4e-b7c2-d43590d9da42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479384} {:answer/id #uuid "3bf77277-da5c-508b-b19f-23412ad069a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479447} {:answer/id #uuid "3c735127-556e-521a-a97e-a560cb0e5a28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479448} {:answer/id #uuid "5a40eb2d-45dd-5e4f-bfc4-ce7f311e8219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479487} {:answer/id #uuid "5d6b668e-9dfd-55fa-bd2b-afca9acc2e9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\sqrt{2}+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479492}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a40eb2d-45dd-5e4f-bfc4-ce7f311e8219" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479487} :answer-field/id #uuid "87c2325f-89dd-5faa-85c5-6f78e19a16ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479786}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4fd8f651-0427-56fb-a0df-c03def6f1c03" :question/math-academy-id "q-94874" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\sqrt{2}$ given that $$\n\\displaystyle ∇f(x,y)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y})i+\\frac{x}{x+y}\\,j\n$$ and $r(t)=2t\\,i+(1-2t)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+\\frac{x}{x+y})i+\\frac{x}{x+y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=1-2t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2t+1-2t)+\\frac{2t}{2t+1-2t})i+\\frac{2t}{2t+1-2t}\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2t)\\,j \\\\  & =2\\,i+(-2)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2t\\,i+2t\\,j)\\cdot (2\\,i+(-2)\\,j) \\\\  & =2t\\cdot 2+2t\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =4t-4t \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\sqrt{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\sqrt{2}))] & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479859 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "275babd6-f291-5f51-9ada-2d31636b381f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479417} {:answer/id #uuid "5e27ed02-56c4-5c23-9e9b-e839f9be174b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479496} {:answer/id #uuid "7d0375e9-56a8-5366-9c6f-1cacf6261aca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479543} {:answer/id #uuid "ceaa9304-14fa-5f01-a4f8-01269d44b5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479676} {:answer/id #uuid "f416028e-1e7f-5522-8bc3-1c8b6b7f478d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479722}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceaa9304-14fa-5f01-a4f8-01269d44b5c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479676} :answer-field/id #uuid "8891d227-4f4f-5b72-a172-ba46661cec18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479787}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "608efbbd-2dcf-58ab-9644-3b94a19193a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-167407" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{xy}$ and $r(t)={t}^{3}\\,i+t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{xy})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{xy})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{y}}{2\\sqrt{x}}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{2\\sqrt{y}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{3}$ and $y=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{t}}{2\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}\\,i+\\frac{\\sqrt{{t}^{3}}}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j)\\cdot (3{t}^{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{2t}+\\frac{t}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}t+\\frac{1}{2}t \\\\  & =2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479863 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bcd1713-5761-536d-9be9-d421928b31e1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479377} {:answer/id #uuid "49414ddc-847d-5122-866b-0471fb4d5ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479467} {:answer/id #uuid "84115f86-a2a8-5473-a1e9-46a7c9992127" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479553} {:answer/id #uuid "85155c3e-a79b-505c-a718-14f3a3535182" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479556} {:answer/id #uuid "c9baafd5-f541-5292-ba8c-6d10037ebe04" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\pi +2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479666}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49414ddc-847d-5122-866b-0471fb4d5ffe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{26}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479467} :answer-field/id #uuid "a6277da0-e2f4-5895-b1b1-64bd47ad216a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479795}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "68446125-1b3e-53ed-aad0-d4e0129128a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-94885" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,3t⟩$ at the point $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x+y))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(x+y)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(x+y)^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=3t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(2t+3t)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(2t+3t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+(5t)^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+(5t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,3⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}},\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}⟩\\cdot ⟨2,3⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2+\\frac{1}{1+25{t}^{2}}\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+25{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =\\frac{5}{1+25(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{1+25} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479876 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ed77687-b07d-532f-8400-2a71d4a5ad46" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479380} {:answer/id #uuid "74be835c-e26b-5172-ac8b-75460bf573b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479537} {:answer/id #uuid "8e9ac26e-5d8d-5f86-b225-32abc20a36ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479573} {:answer/id #uuid "c5a561f8-22fa-5008-be18-18d377fa405a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479658} {:answer/id #uuid "caa5e437-184f-52d9-8cfa-224935e09da8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479667}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e9ac26e-5d8d-5f86-b225-32abc20a36ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479573} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab9b2392-4e70-5610-9864-41016bfde623" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479796}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "99c3ab32-c332-5aab-98f3-193b3fead123" :question/math-academy-id "q-167500" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\frac{1}{t},t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z)),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(xy+z))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z),-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy+z)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2},y=\\frac{1}{t},$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{1}{t}\\right),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}},1⟩ \\\\  & =(-\\frac{1}{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot 2t+(-{t}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\right)+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))] & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4})=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479880 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15e8a119-89e7-5e83-8ece-e6e69dba1106" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t-2){e}^{{t}^{2}+t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479393} {:answer/id #uuid "571e0ae0-7b23-51f2-b237-5930ff5b4a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}+t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479484} {:answer/id #uuid "68fdb16f-c294-542e-b24f-3fcf75971b20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2t{e}^{{t}^{2}+t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479515} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2b5ef5-5b34-543c-ac98-cc2b1b5278c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{t}^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479637} {:answer/id #uuid "cc9e20ec-6a55-5371-95d9-8a1fc2f05681" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}-t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479671}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "571e0ae0-7b23-51f2-b237-5930ff5b4a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}+t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479484} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c59d1a9-7623-5670-96a8-dca459663150" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479788}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aa703c37-d0d3-5fc5-8021-cece98eb14f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-167406" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={e}^{x-y}$ and $r(t)={t}^{2}\\,i-t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}{e}^{x-y}\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}{e}^{x-y}\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{x-y}\\,i-{e}^{x-y}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=-t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,i-{e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i-\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,i-{e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\,j)\\cdot (2t\\,i-\\,j) \\\\  & ={e}^{{t}^{2}+t}\\cdot 2t+(-{e}^{{t}^{2}+t})\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =(2t+1){e}^{{t}^{2}+t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479883 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "032cc53a-d445-5e45-affb-dbc26bb23949" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3+3t-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479365} {:answer/id #uuid "38580c3a-82d9-598a-afe3-0eb7825be631" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t-{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479443} {:answer/id #uuid "446c6267-5187-59df-b71e-b14bacbbc752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2+4t-3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479459} {:answer/id #uuid "c876a545-69bd-57fe-b996-cb8971e263d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479663} {:answer/id #uuid "cb391fc8-c0cb-57ad-8f3d-ae952561b48a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-4t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479669}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c876a545-69bd-57fe-b996-cb8971e263d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2-3{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479663} :answer-field/id #uuid "f5d24599-ab4c-5c66-8f82-5120438263f0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5969d89-d7a6-55d3-97d8-1ebc95c98dd0" :question/math-academy-id "q-95053" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+xy$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i-{t}^{2}\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+xy)\\,j \\\\  & =(2x+y)\\,i+x\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t+1$ and $y=-{t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2t+2-{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\,i-2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =((2t+2-{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\,i-2t\\,j) \\\\  & =2t+2-{t}^{2}-2t(t+1) \\\\  & =2t+2-{t}^{2}-2{t}^{2}-2t \\\\  & =2-3{t}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479889 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ddc5bf6-629f-5b9e-90fa-568c3a604a38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479407} {:answer/id #uuid "8496cb5b-1076-5966-83ce-efcde2ebfdc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479554} {:answer/id #uuid "eb2317df-8f4f-5424-b64c-0f6b65e5bd2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{e}^{2}-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479706} {:answer/id #uuid "f6cb2b76-1e16-50f5-9407-75051eebbee1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479728} {:answer/id #uuid "f95e9936-472c-5c49-aaf5-2f9eb5565841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479734}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8496cb5b-1076-5966-83ce-efcde2ebfdc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4+{e}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479554} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0c8c22e-7185-5961-8bbd-34aaa39fe635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479810}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "c6083028-59c6-57ef-95a8-453f1847efa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94889" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{e}^{y}+z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨4,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,{e}^{t}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{e}^{y}+z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{e}^{y}+z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{e}^{y}+z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{y},x{e}^{y},1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=4,$ $y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t},4{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t},1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨t,4t,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t),{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\frac{1}{t},{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨t,4t,1⟩\\cdot ⟨0,\\frac{1}{t},{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\  & =t\\cdot 0+4t\\cdot \\frac{1}{t}+1\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =4+{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =4+{e}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479899 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a625792-3ebb-5931-8a2d-6a88c5f87ba5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}-2\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479401} {:answer/id #uuid "2f3fec76-0a6e-5e27-983b-461d88932bfb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t\\operatorname{cot}⁡t+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{tan}⁡{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479430} {:answer/id #uuid "a28056a9-f916-52a5-9c57-bf67a29a290d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479603} {:answer/id #uuid "b34fbe3e-3237-5a50-aaa6-a8cad66ce7ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479626} {:answer/id #uuid "bce18e25-d9a2-5b97-b848-b56b779ae552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bce18e25-d9a2-5b97-b848-b56b779ae552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479644} :answer-field/id #uuid "73405ecf-58c2-52c0-a13d-a399aaa1b75c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479781}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "f5e2cc69-b41b-55a6-ba1d-f0aa44f4db50" :question/math-academy-id "q-95143" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)$ and $r(t)=t\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z)\\,k \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,i-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,j+\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\,i-\\operatorname{tan}⁡y\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡z\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y={t}^{2}$ and $z={e}^{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,k \\\\  & =i+2t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cot}⁡t\\,i-\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}\\,j+\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}\\,k)\\cdot (i+2t\\,j+{e}^{t}\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{tan}⁡{t}^{2}+{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cot}⁡{e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186479902 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e692ef2-7942-50c0-987c-003e0499c7d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479379} {:answer/id #uuid "5dbb788f-4b96-5fa4-a950-81307ab26f6a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\pi }^{2}+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479494} {:answer/id #uuid "7ca40bf8-6377-5d1a-9803-7cbf03f171f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479542} {:answer/id #uuid "c35cedf1-0de5-5562-b74c-d3e0a68630e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479655} {:answer/id #uuid "fc35db42-0a91-517b-8f1b-307df57fe44c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c35cedf1-0de5-5562-b74c-d3e0a68630e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi +2}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479655} :answer-field/id #uuid "5689c88b-fb4c-5488-beac-e34a9824569a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186479772}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "fc2162e8-e3d2-5905-8722-73fb096c219a" :question/math-academy-id "q-94880" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{x}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,j$ and $r(t)=({t}^{2}+1)\\,i+t\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{x}{1+{y}^{2}}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={t}^{2}+1$ and $y=t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{{t}^{2}+1}{1+{t}^{2}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,i+j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\,i+j)\\cdot (2t\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t\\cdot 2t+1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2t\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(1))] & =2(1)\\operatorname{arctan}⁡1+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi +2}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186485588 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "131e1312-3232-541a-a772-9a4195876e08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484854} {:answer/id #uuid "1cf61e8f-1cbe-5cf7-b7de-cb285c2945df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186484868} {:answer/id #uuid "54a8785f-b684-5fbf-ab5e-7a2651308e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485006} {:answer/id #uuid "7b1155ed-ffcf-5482-9835-4e132b93d976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2{t}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485086} {:answer/id #uuid "7d4e5d76-01d4-5027-9d0b-5f4455a3c94c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54a8785f-b684-5fbf-ab5e-7a2651308e2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485006} :answer-field/id #uuid "26f9db34-f1fa-54a6-9839-7f297b6a756c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186485384}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "eabe5e6a-dc85-5118-84d2-9aa9f31a6a23" :question/math-academy-id "q-95086" :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)$ and $r(t)=(t+1)\\,i+2t\\,j+{t}^{2}\\,k,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y,z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z))\\,k \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(y+z)\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,j+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(y+z)\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t+1,$ $y=2t$ and $z={t}^{2}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+1)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,k \\\\  & =\\,i+2\\,j+2t\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,i+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,j+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\,k)\\cdot (\\,i+2\\,j+2t\\,k) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 1+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2+(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2t(t+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)(1+t)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t+{t}^{2})+2(t+1)^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t+{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186515530 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0618c56b-92f8-5743-9e32-328933507a90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{t}+{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514887} {:answer/id #uuid "42831ed2-f44c-506e-a16d-0e06ead786c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}-{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515014} {:answer/id #uuid "581a6ccb-d929-5a4c-abd5-314717c764d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}+t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515068} {:answer/id #uuid "b182ff13-9dbc-5c38-8d5b-05e109460201" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515243} {:answer/id #uuid "b6bd0e4e-b413-5783-9897-cec63d3177bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{t}+t}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515258}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "581a6ccb-d929-5a4c-abd5-314717c764d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2t}+t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515068} :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb54adf-64fe-5825-9ef0-62336c5f858a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186515471}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "2becf284-e142-5ae3-80f6-d208696ea202" :question/math-academy-id "q-95068" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}$ and $r(t)={e}^{t}\\,i+t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{y}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}}\\,j\\text{ }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{t}$ and $y=t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & ={e}^{t}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,i+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}\\,j)\\cdot ({e}^{t}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}+\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t}+t}{\\sqrt{{e}^{2t}+{t}^{2}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536110 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "09dbf362-0ea5-5f52-a5a9-966c3c7d12bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(2 e^{2 t} + \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536102} {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fe6f8-cd4f-5ff8-9a88-a56b4315d98e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536122} {:answer/id #uuid "742a3fd6-b9c4-5147-a632-39b718c9a4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536138} {:answer/id #uuid "9011cc4b-f8e1-5639-a6a0-59f04c5aa9e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\left(- \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536148} {:answer/id #uuid "9a209fc2-c827-5e3f-995a-0fc60aebe583" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 \\left(e^{4 t} + \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d3fe6f8-cd4f-5ff8-9a88-a56b4315d98e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4 \\left(\\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(4 t \\right)}\\right) e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} - 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{2 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 2 e^{t} \\sin{\\left(4 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536122} :answer-field/id #uuid "78c7be4e-6cd5-5ed1-a27a-c153bf398a09" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536139}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "1cb8adf3-5059-5402-b886-e9c6e0d73f1e" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(2x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2{y}^{2}}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4xy}{1+4{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{2t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{2}}{1+4({e}^{2t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{4({e}^{2t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)}{1+4({e}^{2t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t)\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{2\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,i+\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}\\,j)\\cdot (2{e}^{2t}\\,i+4\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{4{e}^{2t}\\sin^{2}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}+\\frac{16{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{2t}(2\\sin^{2}⁡4t+8\\operatorname{sin}⁡4t\\operatorname{cos}⁡4t)}{1+4{e}^{4t}\\sin^{4}⁡4t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536116 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a57213b-4fa3-5117-81e3-a2d1f931c7b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536103} {:answer/id #uuid "0f22572f-894b-53df-a836-434bde50cdac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3 e^{3 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536105} {:answer/id #uuid "6cda11da-4b3d-5818-8261-a86fabce7b19" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\left(2 e^{3 t} + \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536137} {:answer/id #uuid "c38550f7-4952-57df-9ae9-77ce137fab61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(3 e^{6 t} + \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536162} {:answer/id #uuid "cd04a189-9235-59c8-a370-b182647b1c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536164}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd04a189-9235-59c8-a370-b182647b1c61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 4 \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)}\\right) e^{3 t} \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)}}{e^{6 t} \\sin^{4}{\\left(2 t \\right)} + 1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536164} :answer-field/id #uuid "a578ac36-cb48-5e2b-b8a0-622f82cf0b66" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536153}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3e7834dc-d7fc-52ab-b7ab-d76e42910958" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2})$ and $r(t)={e}^{3t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\,j,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(x{y}^{2}))\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{(1{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 0)}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{(0{y}^{2}+x2y\\cdot 1)}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2xy}{1+{x}^{2}{y}^{4}}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={e}^{3t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}}{1+({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,i+\\frac{2({e}^{3t})(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)}{1+({e}^{3t})^{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{4}}\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{3t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)\\,j \\\\  & =3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\frac{\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,i+\\frac{2{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}\\,j)\\cdot (3{e}^{3t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t\\,j) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3t}\\sin^{2}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}+\\frac{4{e}^{3t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3t}(3\\sin^{2}⁡2t+4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)}{1+{e}^{6t}\\sin^{4}⁡2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186536131 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cded62a-f1b3-5de4-935c-37ceeec7974e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2 \\left(8 e^{8 t} + 3 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536104} {:answer/id #uuid "155e3333-446f-5203-8b48-f118c022d0a8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "- \\frac{4 \\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 3 \\cos{\\left(3 t \\right)}\\right) e^{4 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186536106} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8442c5-7b76-56f1-8d9d-dbbf442f8af5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)}}{\\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{\\left(3 t \\right)} - 2 e^{2 t} \\sin{\\left(3 t \\right)} + 1\\right) \\left(2 e^{4 t} \\sin^{2}{